WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.410
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:10.300
Today we're going to talk about a rigorous
scientific topic, none other than the creation

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.340
of the perfect selfie photo.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.539
By definition selfies are made by us, which
means that these are typically short-range

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:22.890
photos, and due to the perspective distortion
of the camera lens, we often experience unpleasant

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:27.080
effects like the heavy magnification the nose
and the forehead.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.810
And get this, this technique enables us to
take a photo and after that, edit the perceived

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.340
camera distance for it without changing anything
else.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.610
Basically, algorithmic beautification!

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:44.450
This technique works the following way: we
analyze the photo and try to figure out how

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:47.970
distant the camera was when the photo was
taken.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:53.170
Then, we create a digital model of the perspective
camera and create a 3D model of the face.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.310
This is a process that mathematicians like
to call fitting.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:02.530
It means that if we know the optics of perspective
cameras, we can work backwards from the input

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:07.610
photo that we have, and find an appropriate
setup that would result in this photo.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:12.990
Then, we will be able to adjust this distance
to even out the camera lens distortions.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:19.000
But that's not all, because as we have a digital
3D model of the face, we can do even more.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.880
For instance, we can also rotate it around
in multiple directions.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.370
To build such a 3D model, we typically try
to locate several well-recognizable hotspots

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:35.040
on the face, such as the chin, eyebrows, nose
stem, the region under the nose, eyes and

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.040
lips.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:41.210
However, as these hotspots lead to a poor
3D representation of the human face, the authors

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.380
added a few more of these hotspots to the
detection process.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.360
This still takes less than 5 seconds.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.030
Earlier, we also talked about a neural network-based
technique that judged our selfie photos by

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:54.760
assigning a score to them.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.090
I would absolutely love to see how that work
would react to a before and after photo that

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.380
comes from this technique.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.630
This way, we could formulate this score as
a maximization problem, and as a result, we

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:12.640
could have an automated technique that truly
creates the perfect selfie photo through these

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.260
warping operations.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:19.430
The best kind of evaluation is when we let
reality be our judge, and use images that

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:25.300
were taken closer or farther away and compare
the output of this technique against them.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.060
These true images bear the ground truth label
throughout this video.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:34.130
The differences are often barely perceptible,
and to provide a better localization of the

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.980
error, some difference images are shown in
the paper.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.670
If you are into stereoscopy, there is also
an entire section about that as well.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:47.120
The authors also uploaded an interactive version
of their work online that anyone can try,

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:48.120
free of charge.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.270
So as always, your scholarly before and after
selfie experiments are more than welcome in

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:54.670
the comments section.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:58.840
Whether you are already subscribed to the
